. Solubility in water (g/100 mL): 2.7 (25 °C); approximately 30 (100 °C). pH of 2% aqueous solution 6.5 to 7.1, more stable than the hydrochloride; suitable for enrichment of flours and feeds, multivitamin preparations /Mononitrate/\n\nO'Neil, M.J. (ed.). The Merck Index - An Encyclopedia of Chemicals, Drugs, and Biologicals. Whitehouse Station, NJ: Merck and Co., Inc., 2006., p. 1598 (PCLID 900134375)\n\nHazardous Substances Data Bank (HSDB)\n\nSMALL WHITE CRYSTALS OR CRYSTALLINE POWDER /HYDROCHLORIDE/\n\nOsol, A. and J.E. Hoover, et al. (eds.). Remington's Pharmaceutical Sciences. 15th ed. Easton, Pennsylvania: Mack Publishing Co., 1975., p. 961 (PCLID 900085609)\n\nHazardous Substances Data Bank (HSDB)\n\nThiamine hydrochloride in the dry state is stable. Acidic solutions having a pH below 5.5 preferably from 5.0 to 3.5 are also relatively stable. Alkalies destroy it. It is precipitated from solution by several of the alkaloidal reagents such as mercuric chloride, iodine, picric acid, tannin, and Mayer's reagent. It is sensitive to both oxidizing and reducing agents. /Hydrochloride/\n\nChase et al; Remington's Pharmaceutical Sciences 14th ed. Mack Publ Co. Easton, PA p. 1040 (1970) (PCLID 900091949)\n\nHazardous Substances Data Bank (HSDB)\n\n\nMedicine, nutrition, enriched flours. Isolated usually as the chloride ... Available as thiamine hydrochloride and thiamine mononitrate\n\nLewis, R.J. Sr.; Hawley's Condensed Chemical Dictionary 15th Edition. John Wiley & Sons, Inc. New York, NY 2007., p. 1234 (PCLID 900132192)\n\nHazardous Substances Data Bank (HSDB)\n\nReported uses\n\nTable: Reported uses (ppm): (Flavor and Extract Manufacturers' Association)\n\nFood Category\n\nUsual\n\nMax\n\nFood Category\n\nBreakfast cereals\n\nUsual\n\n50.00\n\nMax\n\n50.00\n\nFood Category\n\nCondiments, relishes\n\nUsual\n\n50.00\n\nMax\n\n50.00\n\nFood Category\n\nMilk products\n\nUsual\n\n50.00\n\nMax\n\n50.00\n\nFood Category\n\nNonalcoholic beverages\n\nUsual\n\n50.00\n\nMax\